  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Water, Plumbing & Electrical",
    tags: [
      "best portable washer",
      "portable washer",
      "portable washing machine",
      "apartment washer",
      "portable washer and dryer for an apartment",
      "twin tub washer"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The Best Portable Washers
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          A portable washer can end the laundromat run, but only if it hooks up to your faucet, drains where you need it to and fits your lease. Here are six worth buying and the plumbing details that decide which one works in your space.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">11 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#what-matters" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>What Actually Matters</span></a>
              <a href="#twin-vs-auto" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Twin Tub vs Fully Automatic</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>The Six Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Comparison Table</span></a>
              <a href="#hookup" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Faucet Adapters and Water Supply</span></a>
              <a href="#drain" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Drain Height and Drain Pumps</span></a>
              <a href="#capacity" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Capacity in Cubic Feet</span></a>
              <a href="#lease-power" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Leases, Floors and Power</span></a>
              <a href="#which-to-buy" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Which One to Buy</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A portable washer is not a small version of a full-size machine. It is a different tool with its own trade-offs: smaller loads, a temporary hookup to a sink, and a drain hose you have to think about every time. Get the hookup and the drain right and a good one will save you hours a week. Get them wrong and it becomes a heavy box in the closet.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters in a Portable Washer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with where the water comes from and where it goes. Every portable washer needs a supply, usually a kitchen or bathroom faucet through a quick-connect adapter, and a drain, usually the same sink, a tub or a standpipe. If your faucet cannot take an adapter, or your sink sits higher than the machine can pump, nothing else on the spec sheet matters. Look at your faucet and measure from the floor to the rim of the sink you plan to drain into before you shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next is capacity, which manufacturers quote in cubic feet of drum volume and sometimes in pounds of dry laundry. Portable machines run from under 1 cu ft to around 2 cu ft. For scale, a typical full-size top-loader is well over 4 cu ft. A 1.6 cu ft machine handles a few days of clothes for one or two people; a 0.9 cu ft machine is closer to a single outfit plus towels. Buy for how often you are willing to run it, not for the biggest number you can fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then decide how hands-on you want to be. A fully automatic portable fills, washes, rinses, spins and drains on its own once it is hooked up. A twin tub is cheaper and simpler but makes you fill it, move clothes from the wash tub to the spin tub and drain it yourself. Both clean clothes well. The difference is whether you can walk away from it, which matters more than most buyers expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the space itself. Portable washers sit on casters or feet and get rolled to the sink on laundry day, so you need a path, a level spot near the faucet and a floor that can take a little water. Upstairs apartments, older buildings and RVs add a layer of risk: a leak or a drain hose that pops out of the sink is your problem and possibly your neighbor's. A cheap drip pan and a habit of staying nearby solve most of that.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you also need to dry clothes indoors, plan the pair together. A compact washer leaves clothes wetter than a full-size machine with a fast spin, which affects drying time on a rack or in a small dryer. Our <a href="/best-portable-dryer/">guide to portable dryers</a> covers the 120V compact dryers that pair with these washers, and the venting rules that come with them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="twin-vs-auto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Twin Tub vs Fully Automatic: Which Type Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A twin tub washer has two compartments side by side. The larger one agitates clothes in water you add with a hose or bucket; the smaller one is a spin basket that wrings the water out after washing and again after rinsing. You set a timer on each side, move the clothes by hand between stages and drain the wash tub through a hose. It sounds primitive, and it is, but it is also cheap, light, quick and very forgiving about water supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            The strengths of a twin tub are real. Because you control the water, you can fill it from any faucet with a hose, from a bucket, or from an RV tank, and you can reuse rinse water if you are conserving. The separate spinner is often the better part of the machine: owners commonly report that clothes come out of a twin tub spinner noticeably drier than out of a small automatic, which shortens drying time. Most twin tubs drain by gravity, which keeps them simple but limits where you can put the drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            A fully automatic portable works like a scaled-down top-loader. You connect it to the faucet with a quick-connect adapter, put the drain hose in the sink, pick a cycle and leave. It handles fill, wash, rinse, spin and drain in sequence, usually with a few water levels and cycles for delicates, heavy items and quick washes. It costs more and weighs more, and it depends entirely on that faucet connection, but it gives you back your time.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most apartment dwellers with a standard kitchen faucet, a fully automatic machine is the better buy. You start it, go do something else and come back to finished laundry. For RVers, campers, people without a compatible faucet and anyone on a tight budget, a twin tub makes more sense. It also suits people who wash small loads frequently, such as workout gear or baby clothes, because each run is short and you can stop at any point.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy automatic for convenience, twin tub for flexibility.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your faucet takes a standard adapter and you want to walk away from the machine, buy fully automatic. If you need to fill from a bucket or tank, drain by gravity, or keep the cost and weight down, buy a twin tub and accept that you will be standing next to it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

