        <div id="lease-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Leases, Floors, and Running on a Generator or Power Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read your lease before you buy. Many leases prohibit washing machines outright or require written permission, mostly because of water damage risk. A portable machine that uses the sink is less risky than a permanent hookup, and many landlords will approve it if you ask. Get the approval in writing, and check that your renter's insurance covers water damage you cause, because a leak in an upstairs unit can damage the apartment below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the floor. Set the washer on a drip pan or a rubber mat, especially on wood, laminate or carpet. A washer pan with a lip will catch a small leak or a drain hose slip. Stay in the apartment while it runs; a small machine still uses several gallons per fill, and that is enough to soak a subfloor if the hose comes loose. When you are done, wipe out the drum and leave the lid open so it dries and does not grow mildew.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrically, a portable washer is a light load. It plugs into an ordinary 120V outlet and draws far less than a dryer or a space heater. Plug it into a grounded outlet, ideally a GFCI-protected one near the sink, as many kitchens and bathrooms already have. Avoid light-duty extension cords near water. If the outlet near your sink is not GFCI-protected, a licensed electrician can add protection, and in a rental that is the landlord's job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running a washer off grid is very doable. A modest portable power station can usually run a small washer, but motors pull a surge on start and spin, so check the watt rating on the washer's label and leave headroom on the station's continuous and surge ratings. A machine with a heater is a different story, because the heater draws much more. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a> covers sizing, and RVers will find the 12V side in our <a href="/best-power-inverter/">power inverter guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            A generator works too, but only outdoors. Never run a gas or propane generator indoors, in a garage, in an RV's living space or near open windows and doors, because carbon monoxide can kill. Run a heavy-duty outdoor-rated cord from the generator to the washer, and never plug a generator into a wall outlet to power the house. Inverter generators give cleaner power for electronic controls; our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator guide</a> covers the quiet inverter models that suit campsites.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Portable Washer to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you live in an apartment with a standard kitchen or bathroom faucet and want the machine to run itself, buy the BLACK+DECKER BPWM16W. It is the most widely available 1.6 cu ft fully automatic portable, it comes with the quick-connect faucet adapter, and it is big enough for one or two people without being a chore to move. The Magic Chef MCSTCW16W1 is the alternative at the same size if the BLACK+DECKER is out of stock or the price is better.
          </p>

          <p>
            If space is the real limit, such as a studio closet, a dorm or a small RV with a shore-power hookup, buy the BLACK+DECKER BPWM09W. It does the same job in a smaller footprint, at the cost of smaller loads. If you want an automatic machine and value matters most, the Panda PAN56MGP3 is the budget automatic to compare, but confirm its capacity and drain method on the listing before you order.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not have a compatible faucet, need to fill from a bucket or tank, or want the lowest price and the driest spin, buy the Giantex Twin Tub. It asks more of you at each step, but it is light, flexible and a good match for RVs and tiny houses. If you have no power at all, or wash delicates and small loads at camp, the Laundry Alternative Wonderwash does a surprising amount with a hand crank and a bucket of water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the very cheapest no-name automatic washers on marketplaces with thin listings and unclear model numbers. They are hard to get parts for, and if the pump or the control board fails, the machine is scrap. A known brand with a real model number and a manual you can download costs a little more and is far easier to keep running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you buy, budget for a drip pan, a lint trap for the drain hose and, if your faucet is unusual, the right adapter. Those accessories cost very little and prevent most of the problems that make people give up on portable washers. If you need a matching dryer, our <a href="/best-portable-dryer/">portable dryer guide</a> covers the 120V compact options and the venting they need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking the faucet first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pull-out sprayers and unusual aerators often will not take the included quick-connect adapter. Unscrew your aerator before you buy and confirm the thread type. If it does not fit, plan on a bathroom faucet, a utility sink or a properly installed supply tee, or buy a twin tub you can fill by hose or bucket.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the drain height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pump can only lift water so high, and a gravity drain cannot lift it at all. Measure the sink rim, check the manual's maximum drain height and the drain method, and choose the drain point before the machine arrives. A drain that cannot keep up leaves water in the drum and on the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the drain hose loose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hose hooked over the sink edge can jump out when the pump starts and dump a drum of water on the floor. Clip or strap it in place every time, add a lint trap and stay nearby while the machine drains, especially in an upstairs apartment where a leak becomes the neighbor's problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overloading a small drum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cramming a 0.9 or 1.6 cu ft drum leaves soap in the clothes, unbalances the spin and strains the motor. Load loosely so items can move in the water, split heavy towels and jeans across loads, and use the spin-only setting if clothes still come out too wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the lease and insurance check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many leases restrict washing machines, and water damage you cause is your liability. Ask your landlord in writing, confirm your renter's insurance covers accidental water damage and use a drip pan. A five-minute conversation now beats a long dispute over a stained ceiling in the unit downstairs later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

